 ad):\\n- Follow the instructions by the director.\\n- Make the ad design, layout, typography on-branded, but should look premium, editorial-like.\\n- Establish a clear visual hierarchy: one dominant focal point the eye lands on\\n  first, then the supporting message, then the CTA.\\n- Keep strong figure/ground contrast. If text sits over imagery, place a scrim or\\n  overlay behind it so it stays fully legible.\\n- Place the focal subject on a rule-of-thirds line; leave generous whitespace and\\n  avoid clutter.\\n- Use a limited palette anchored to the brand colors (~60-30-10), reserving one\\n  accent color for the call to action.\\n- Keep 20% space all around the creative for the ad to look cleaner\\n- Group and align elements cleanly (Gestalt); use at most two type styles.\",\"status\":\"generating\",\"createdBy\":\"f5dfb377-dac7-46a6-aea3-a4e04cb45e54\",\"metadata\":{\"brief\":\"Using the PAS (Problem-Agitation-Solution) framework, this discovery ad targets people suffering from chronic iron-deficiency fatigue. The visual layout is a stark, split-screen contrast: the left half is a dim, cool-toned photograph of a woman slumped at her work desk at 2:00 PM, overwhelmed by brain fog and surrounded by three empty coffee mugs; the right half is a clean, warm Sand Beige block featuring a 3D render of the transparent capsule filled with dusty-pink micro-pellets. This layout creates an instant pattern interrupt in the feed by highlighting the familiar frustration of caffeine-resistant afternoon fatigue. The bold headline, \\\"Tired of feeling constantly tired?\\\", is styled in the premium, geometric sans-serif Circular Std in Premium Black, occupying under 10% of the overall layout. The soft CTA at the bottom reads \\\"learn more\\\" in a sharp, rectangular pill-shaped button with a 0px border radius using the primary brand orange to draw the eye. To keep the ad on-brand, ensure the lifestyle side transitions smoothly to the warm, natural Sand Beige side, use the cle